1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:06,600
Welcome back to the ERM platform
demonstration series. In

2
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:09,800
this second demonstration, you'll watch a
Risk Manager

3
00:00:09,800 --> 00:00:14,200
propose a formal treatment plan for an
existing risk, and a

4
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:18,200
Chief Risk Officer decide it — the same
governed pattern

5
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:22,200
from Demonstration 01, now applied to a
different kind of

6
00:00:22,200 --> 00:00:25,800
decision: not just scoring a risk, but
committing the

7
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:29,400
organization to a specific, dated plan to
reduce it.

8
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:36,400
A quick reminder, for anyone joining this
series for the

9
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:39,600
first time here: Meridian Asset Management
Limited keeps

10
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:43,200
every identified risk in one structured
register, each with

11
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:48,400
an owner, a score, and a review date — and
every change to

12
00:00:48,400 --> 00:00:51,600
that register runs through the same
governed maker-checker

13
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:55,200
approval you saw in Demonstration 01.
Today's addition to

14
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:59,200
that picture is risk treatment: the formal
record of what

15
00:00:59,200 --> 00:01:03,600
the organization is actually going to do
about a risk, not

16
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:05,200
just how it's scored.

17
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:11,400
You already know today's two participants
from Demonstration

18
00:01:11,400 --> 00:01:15,400
01. Arjun Mehta, Meridian's Risk Manager,
is once again the

19
00:01:15,400 --> 00:01:19,800
maker — this time proposing a treatment
plan rather than a

20
00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:20,200
re-assessment.

21
00:01:20,200 --> 00:01:23,400
And Priya Raghunathan, Meridian's Chief
Risk Officer, is

22
00:01:23,400 --> 00:01:27,400
once again the checker — the same
separation of duties,

23
00:01:27,400 --> 00:01:30,200
applied to a new type of decision.

24
00:01:33,200 --> 00:01:36,400
Arjun opens the same risk from
Demonstration 01:

25
00:01:36,400 --> 00:01:39,600
RSK-2026-0004, credit default of a
portfolio issuer below

26
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:43,200
investment grade — still Active, still
scored 10 inherent

27
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:47,200
and 8 residual. One control already exists
against it: an

28
00:01:47,200 --> 00:01:50,400
issuer credit review and watchlist
process, effective but

29
00:01:50,400 --> 00:01:55,200
not, on its own, enough to satisfy Arjun
that this risk is

30
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:56,800
being actively driven down.

31
00:01:56,800 --> 00:02:00,400
On the Relationships tab, the picture is
clear: zero

32
00:02:00,400 --> 00:02:04,800
treatment plans exist yet for this risk. A
control tells you

33
00:02:04,800 --> 00:02:08,800
what's watching the risk today. A
treatment plan tells you

34
00:02:08,800 --> 00:02:13,200
what the organization is doing to actually
reduce it — and

35
00:02:13,200 --> 00:02:14,800
right now, nothing is.

36
00:02:17,800 --> 00:02:22,200
Arjun opens a new Treatment Plan. He
selects a strategy —

37
00:02:22,200 --> 00:02:25,400
Mitigate, one of four options the platform
supports

38
00:02:25,400 --> 00:02:29,000
alongside Accept, Transfer, and Avoid —
names it Enhanced

39
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:31,800
issuer credit monitoring and watchlist
escalation, sets

40
00:02:31,800 --> 00:02:35,400
himself as owner, targets bringing the
residual score down

41
00:02:35,400 --> 00:02:40,200
from 8 to 6, and sets a due date of 30
November.

42
00:02:40,200 --> 00:02:44,600
He saves it. The platform assigns it a
code — TRT-2026-0009

43
00:02:44,600 --> 00:02:49,000
— and creates it in Proposed status.
Notice the action bar:

44
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:53,000
Edit, Submit for Approval, Cancel. A
treatment plan can be

45
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:56,600
freely revised while it's still Proposed —
nothing is

46
00:02:56,600 --> 00:03:00,200
governed yet. That changes the moment
Arjun submits it.

47
00:03:03,200 --> 00:03:07,200
Arjun clicks Submit for Approval. Exactly
as with a risk

48
00:03:07,200 --> 00:03:10,800
re-assessment, this is a governed action —
a justification

49
00:03:10,800 --> 00:03:14,400
is mandatory. He explains why: the
existing control hasn't

50
00:03:14,400 --> 00:03:18,000
been tested against a rapid multi-notch
downgrade, and this

51
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:22,000
plan targets closing that gap without
waiting for the next

52
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:22,800
scheduled review.

53
00:03:22,800 --> 00:03:26,800
He submits. The status stays Proposed, but
a new badge

54
00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:29,600
appears: Awaiting checker. And exactly
like Demonstration

55
00:03:29,600 --> 00:03:34,400
01, GOV-07 takes over — one pending action
per record — and

56
00:03:34,400 --> 00:03:38,800
every action button Arjun had a moment ago
is gone. He

57
00:03:38,800 --> 00:03:40,800
cannot approve his own plan.

58
00:03:43,800 --> 00:03:47,400
This is the same governance ledger you
watched in

59
00:03:47,400 --> 00:03:51,400
Demonstration 01, doing exactly the same
job for a different

60
00:03:51,400 --> 00:03:55,400
kind of decision. The rule that blocked
Arjun from deciding

61
00:03:55,400 --> 00:03:59,800
his own risk re-assessment blocks him here
too — because a

62
00:03:59,800 --> 00:04:03,800
treatment plan is just as consequential a
governed change as

63
00:04:03,800 --> 00:04:07,400
a risk score, and self-approval defeats
the point of

64
00:04:07,400 --> 00:04:09,000
independent review either way.

65
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:12,600
We switch personas to Priya Raghunathan.
She opens her

66
00:04:12,600 --> 00:04:15,800
Approvals queue — the same cross-module
queue from

67
00:04:15,800 --> 00:04:19,800
Demonstration 01 — and Arjun's new
submission has landed at

68
00:04:19,800 --> 00:04:24,200
the top, one of seven pending items now
waiting on her.

69
00:04:27,200 --> 00:04:30,800
Priya can see the full justification Arjun
submitted, with

70
00:04:30,800 --> 00:04:34,400
its timestamp, and the same three options
as before:

71
00:04:34,400 --> 00:04:36,800
Approve, Reject, or Return for rework.

72
00:04:36,800 --> 00:04:40,800
She clicks Approve. A decision dialog
opens for an optional

73
00:04:40,800 --> 00:04:44,400
comment. She writes that the added
monitoring and monthly

74
00:04:44,400 --> 00:04:47,600
review cadence are proportionate to the
current outlook

75
00:04:47,600 --> 00:04:50,000
pressure on this issuer, and confirms.

76
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:53,600
She clicks Confirm approve. The governance
ledger closes the

77
00:04:53,600 --> 00:04:58,000
loop exactly as before: the plan moves to
Approved, and both

78
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:00,800
Arjun's justification and Priya's decision
comment are

79
00:05:00,800 --> 00:05:02,800
written permanently to the record.

80
00:05:05,800 --> 00:05:09,800
On the treatment plan's own Approvals tab,
the full decision

81
00:05:09,800 --> 00:05:13,800
is now a single permanent entry: Risk
Treatment Approval —

82
00:05:13,800 --> 00:05:17,000
Approved. Maker: Arjun Mehta, with his
timestamp. Checker:

83
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:19,800
Priya Raghunathan, with hers. Both
statements preserved

84
00:05:19,800 --> 00:05:23,400
verbatim, side by side — the same
defensible record

85
00:05:23,400 --> 00:05:26,600
structure Demonstration 01 established,
now applied to a

86
00:05:26,600 --> 00:05:29,000
treatment commitment instead of a score.

87
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:35,200
Back on RSK-2026-0004's own Relationships
tab, the Treatment

88
00:05:35,200 --> 00:05:38,800
Plans count has moved from zero to one:
TRT-2026-0009,

89
00:05:38,800 --> 00:05:42,000
Mitigate, due 30 November, status Approved
— visible

90
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:45,600
directly on the risk record itself, not
filed away

91
00:05:45,600 --> 00:05:46,000
separately.

92
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:49,200
That's the business outcome. Before this
workflow, Meridian

93
00:05:49,200 --> 00:05:53,600
could say this risk was scored and
controlled. Now it can

94
00:05:53,600 --> 00:05:56,400
also say, with a dated, owned,
independently-approved

95
00:05:56,400 --> 00:06:00,400
commitment: here is exactly what we're
doing to bring it

96
00:06:00,400 --> 00:06:04,800
down further, by when, and who is
accountable if it isn't

97
00:06:04,800 --> 00:06:05,200
done.

98
00:06:05,200 --> 00:06:08,000
You've now watched the platform's governed
maker-checker

99
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:13,200
pattern applied twice — once to a risk
score, once to a risk

100
00:06:13,200 --> 00:06:16,800
treatment plan — through the identical
ledger, the identical

101
00:06:16,800 --> 00:06:20,400
GOV-07 rule, and the identical audit
trail. That consistency

102
00:06:20,400 --> 00:06:24,800
is the point: this is one governance
engine, not a different

103
00:06:24,800 --> 00:06:27,200
approval flow bolted onto every module.

104
00:06:27,200 --> 00:06:30,400
This concludes Demonstration 02. The next
demonstrations in

105
00:06:30,400 --> 00:06:34,000
this series continue through the Risk
module — risk

106
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:37,600
acceptance, and KRI breach escalation —
before moving into

107
00:06:37,600 --> 00:06:40,800
Controls, Compliance, and every other
